{"name":"Aragonite White","hex":"#f3f1f3","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aragonite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evaporite consisting of anhydrous calcium carbonate with the chemical formula CaCO3 and occurring in pearls, shells and nacre; it is dimorphous with calcite."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aragonite"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aragonite","blocks":[{"text":"Aragonite is a carbonate mineral and one of the three most common naturally occurring crystal forms of calcium carbonate, the others being calcite and vaterite. It is formed by biological and physical processes, including precipitation from marine and freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Aragonite_crystal_-_Los_Molinillos%2C_Ceunca%2C_Spain_-_4x3.6x3.5cm_100g.jpg/330px-Aragonite_crystal_-_Los_Molinillos%2C_Ceunca%2C_Spain_-_4x3.6x3.5cm_100g.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aragonite"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],